<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3100 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menes was the king whom later Egyptian tradition and Greek historians credited with uniting Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> around 3100 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> said he founded Memphis. Egyptologists usually identify him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a> or his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hor-aha">Hor-Aha</a>, since the name does not appear clearly on contemporary monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menes.jpg" alt="Menes" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abydos_Königsliste_06.jpg: Olaf Tausch derivative work: JMCC1 (talk) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abydos_KL_01-01_n01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
